---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="The content cache"
  description="Every screen loads its art by calling the same function. That function is the heart of the asset pipeline."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'How the Game Works', href: `${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/` },
    { label: 'Content cache' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    When a screen wants its sprites, it doesn't open files directly. It
    calls a single shared function — the <strong>content cache loader</strong>
    — with a path string and a sub-index. The loader either gives back
    a pointer to the already-loaded asset, or reads the file off the
    cart, parses the container, and caches it for future calls.
  </p>

  <p>
    The pattern looks something like this, paraphrasing the
    decompiled code:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`asset_ptr = load_asset(
    master_table,        // pointer to cache table
    "2d/buyitem.lzs",    // path string
    sub_index,           // which entry inside the container
    size_hint,
    flags,
    type_code,
    ...
);`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Inside, the loader walks two helpers: a "find" function that checks
    if the asset is already in cache, and a "load" function that reads
    it from disk if not. The cache itself is a table indexed by path
    string plus sub-index.
  </p>

  <h2>The master table</h2>
  <p>
    The master cache table lives at a fixed memory address in the ARM9
    binary (<code>0x020AA740</code>). Over 700 functions across the
    ARM9 and overlays reference this address, which is why we know
    it's the central one. Sub-tables hang off it for different kinds
    of assets — sprite cells, palettes, tile sheets.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this exists</h2>
  <p>
    Assets in this game are surprisingly chunky. A typical shop screen
    pulls in a tile sheet plus a palette plus a tilemap plus a sprite
    definition — easily 30 KB once decompressed. If every screen
    re-loaded that data from cart on every entry, transitions would be
    slow. Caching the parsed result means the second time you enter a
    shop is essentially free.
  </p>

  <p>
    The cache also normalizes access. Whether a file is compressed
    (<code>.lzs</code>) or not (<code>.ofs</code>), whether it's
    nested or flat, doesn't matter to the calling code. It hands off a
    path and gets back a pointer to ready-to-use data.
  </p>

  <h2>Path strings as keys</h2>
  <p>
    The path strings themselves live in a few places. Most are in a
    big string pool inside the ARM9 binary — over 130 known paths in
    a single pool. Others are tucked into overlay data sections, so
    that a controller overlay can have its specific file paths handy
    when it loads.
  </p>

  <p>
    When we want to know which overlay loads which file, we search for
    the path string. The result is a map: 50+ container files paired
    with the overlay that requests them. That map is one of the
    handier outputs of the reverse-engineering work — it tells us, for
    instance, that <code>2d/chest.lzs</code> is loaded by overlay
    ov013.
  </p>

  <h2>Pre-wrapped loaders</h2>
  <p>
    For some categories of asset, the ARM9 has a thin wrapper that
    bakes in the file path. The item-icon loader is one — it
    takes a single sub-index argument and internally calls the generic
    cache loader with <code>item/itemicon.ofs</code>. About 16 of
    these category-specific wrappers exist, one for each major asset
    family (item icons, magic glyphs, NPC portraits, etc.).
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
